{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","title":"Slope-intercept form of a straight line"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Slope-intercept form describes a straight line. The number b tells us where the line crosses the vertical axis.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"form","region":"A1","latex":"y=mx+b"},{"op":"text","id":"key","anchor":{"el":"form","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"m: slope     b: vertical intercept"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2,4],"y":[-3,9],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"line","y":"2*x+1","domain":[-2,4]},{"kind":"point","id":"intercept","at":{"on":"line","x":0},"label":"(0, 1)"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Here the vertical intercept is one. Starting at zero, one, move one unit right and two units up to reach another point.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"example","anchor":{"el":"key","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"y=2x+1\\qquad b=1"},{"op":"figure","id":"triangle","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"next","at":{"on":"line","x":1},"label":"(1, 3)"},{"kind":"segment","id":"run","from":{"at":"intercept"},"to":[1,1],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"run = 1"},{"kind":"segment","id":"rise","from":[1,1],"to":{"at":"next"},"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"rise = 2"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Slope is rise divided by run. This line rises two units for every one unit right, so its slope is two.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"slope","anchor":{"el":"example","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"m=\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}=\\frac{2}{1}=2"},{"op":"figure","id":"extend","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"third","at":{"on":"line","x":2},"label":"(2, 5)"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"next"},"to":[2,3],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[2,3],"to":{"at":"third"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"+2"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For y = 2x + 1, what are the slope and vertical intercept?","expected_gist":"Slope 2; vertical intercept 1."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Use the intercept to start, then repeat the slope to find points. At x equals three, this line reaches y equals seven.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"verify","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"fourth","at":{"on":"line","x":3},"label":"(3, 7)"},{"kind":"segment","from":[3,0],"to":{"at":"fourth"},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"check","anchor":{"el":"slope","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=2(3)+1=7"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"7f772f39-47ac-49ee-a1c6-9ca4d19e5a95","accepted_steps":4,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
